response:
 We begin by finding the inverse of our function.  The first thing we do is switch $x$ and $y$: $$x=\frac{a}{y+2}$$ Next, we solve for $y$: \begin{align*}
x(y+2)=a &\Rightarrow xy+2x=a\\
\Rightarrow xy=y(x-2)=a-2x
\end{align*}Thus, $$y=\frac{a-2x}{x-2}$$In other words, the $\text{inverse}$ of our function is just $$f^{-1}(x)=\frac{a-2x}{x-2}$$Now, by the definition of the inverse, $f(0)=f^{-1}(3a)$ implies \begin{align*}
\frac{a}{0+2}=\frac a{3a-2}\\
\Rightarrow\qquad a(3a-2)=2a\\
\Rightarrow\qquad a=\frac23
\end{align*}
